                "The optimization engine evaluates digital interfaces across three rigorous quantitative dimensions: Visual Clutter and Information Entropy, Saliency-Intent Alignment, and Cognitive Processing Load. Visual clutter is calculated using Ruth Rosenholtz's Feature Congestion and Subband Entropy algorithms, which quantify the visual complexity of an interface by measuring localized clutter in luminance, color, and edge orientation across multiple spatial frequency bands. High visual clutter degrades visual search efficiency, increases Time to First Fixation (TTFF) on critical conversion triggers, and forces users into erratic, high-effort visual scanpaths.",
